+### Encoder Training via Synthetic Data
+A method for training an encoder to predict latent codes and canonical maps using data generated from an unconditional generator, avoiding the need for ground-truth 3D meshes or hand-modeled synthetic meshes required by NOCS [S3]. The steps are: 1. Sample a minibatch of priors $z \sim N(0, I)$. 2. Feed priors through a mapping network to obtain latent codes $w \in W$. 3. Train the network (e.g., SegFormer with a custom regression head) to predict the canonical map and latent code $w$ from the RGB image [S3]. A key pitfall in related methods like NOCS is the requirement for ground-truth 3D meshes or hand-modeled synthetic meshes representative of the training dataset, which are not available on real datasets [S3].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** The method is designed to be real-time capable, avoiding the significant time required for inference in other NeRF-based navigation methods that require rendering-based optimization [S1].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the size of the RNR-Map grid and the dimensionality of the latent codes $D$ [S1].
 *   **Strengths:** Enables generalization to unseen environments using pretrained encoder and decoder [S1]. Real-time capability for visual navigation [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Autoencoding a 3D environment is not trivial compared to 2D images [S1]. Requires accurate pose estimation for registration [S1].
